{%- comment -%}
  Florista legal document — built 2026-08-21 for /pages/privacy-policy, and reusable for the
  terms of use and the accessibility statement when those are written. See docs/design-log.md.

  2026-09-05: /pages/privacy-policy is GONE. The owner wants one policy page on the site, and that is
  Shopify's own slot at /policies/privacy-policy (Settings → Policies) — the one checkout and
  the cookie banner link to, which no theme page can replace. The page was deleted and its URL
  redirects there. This section stays for the terms of use and the accessibility statement.

  the owner asked for a privacy policy page shaped like zer4u.co.il's, in Florista's design, with
  readability as the only goal — explicitly not a showpiece. So this section carries no
  photography, no brand-coloured band and no hero: a legal document is read, not browsed, and
  every decoration would be one more thing between the reader and the clause they came for.

  Content/design split, per the standing rule that every element has editable content in the
  admin and coded design in the repo:

    - The DESIGN is here and in florista-legal.css — measure, rules between clauses, the tinted
      contents card, the closing contact block.
    - The TEXT is the Shopify page body, edited in the admin by the owner or a lawyer without a
      deploy. The authored source is kept in docs/legal/privacy-policy.he.html so the version
      that is live is also in git.
    - The FALLBACK below covers the case Shopify guarantees will happen: the template is
      assigned to a page whose body has not been pasted in yet. It says so rather than
      rendering an empty page under a heading.

  The heading levels are load-bearing, not styling. The page title is the only h1; every
  numbered clause in the content is an h2 with an id, and the contents list at the top links to
  those ids. That is what makes a 15-clause document usable on a 375px phone.
{%- endcomment -%}

{{ 'florista-legal.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

{%- liquid
  assign updated = section.settings.updated_label
  assign intro = section.settings.intro
-%}

<section class="fl-legal">
  <div class="fl-legal-inner">
    <h1 class="fl-legal-title">{{ page.title | escape }}</h1>

    {%- if updated != blank -%}
      <p class="fl-legal-updated">{{ updated }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if intro != blank -%}
      <p class="fl-legal-intro">{{ intro }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="fl-legal-body">
      {%- comment -%}
        The empty state used to print Shopify admin instructions — "יש להוסיף אותו בממשק הניהול
        של Shopify, תחת Content ← Pages ← …" — to customers, on an indexable legal page. A note
        to the shop owner does not belong in the storefront: it is confusing to a customer,
        embarrassing on a legal page specifically, and indexable. It is now an HTML comment,
        which the owner still sees in the page source while nobody else does, and the visible empty
        state says nothing at all rather than saying the wrong thing. Fixed 2026-08-30.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if page.content != blank -%}
        {{ page.content }}
      {%- else -%}
        <!-- Florista: this page has no content yet. Add it in Shopify admin under
             Content → Pages → {{ page.title | escape }}. -->
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Florista legal",
  "tag": "section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "updated_label",
      "label": "שורת עדכון אחרון",
      "default": "עודכן לאחרונה: 21 באוגוסט 2026",
      "info": "יש לעדכן בכל שינוי מהותי במסמך. השאירו ריק כדי להסתיר את השורה."
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "intro",
      "label": "משפט פתיחה (אופציונלי)",
      "info": "מוצג בגדול מתחת לכותרת, לפני גוף המסמך."
    }
  ],
  "presets": [{ "name": "Florista legal" }]
}
{% endschema %}
